[49m[38;5;10m+    64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Note on rows 4 and 16 (the two NULL-dereference sites)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `add_node`'s `strncpy` and `process_string`'s `*str` both dereference their
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pointer argument with no NULL check, so `NULL` is undefined behaviour in C. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C `.so` faults with `SIGSEGV`, and so does the **release** Rust `.so` — the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m artifact that corresponds to the C shared library.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A **debug** Rust build additionally carries rustc's `-C debug-assertions` UB
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m checks, which notice the NULL dereference before it faults and panic; a panic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m escaping an `extern "C"` function aborts, so the debug `.so` dies with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIGABRT` instead of `SIGSEGV`. That is the compiler's deliberate tripwire on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input that has no defined behaviour in C either, not a behavioural divergence
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in the translation. `assert_deadly_signals_match` therefore requires:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - C always faults with `SIGSEGV`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - the release Rust `.so` faults with the *same* signal as C;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - the debug Rust `.so` also dies (never returns), with `SIGABRT`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both are verified: `run_all_combos.sh` runs the suite against both profiles.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Divergence found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Phase B/C found exactly one real divergence, in the floating-point accumulation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m of `calculate_subtree_sum`. It is documented in `CONFIGS.md` (rows 45–47) and in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the `addsd` helper in `src/lib.rs`: gcc lowers `sum += calculate_subtree_sum(...)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to `addsd %xmm1,%xmm0` with the **child's** value in the destination register, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m x86 `addsd` returns the destination operand when it is a NaN — so the child's NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sign and payload win over the accumulator's, the opposite of what `sum += child`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m produces in Rust. Reachable from the public API because `add_node` accepts an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m arbitrary `double`.
