[38;5;252m[1m## The divergence[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mcalculate_subtree_sum[0m propagated the wrong NaN when two NaN values met in its floating-point accumulation.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
gcc lowers the C statement [38;5;10msum += calculate_subtree_sum(node_storage[i].id);[0m to [38;5;10maddsd %xmm1,%xmm0[0m, where [38;5;10m%xmm0[0m holds the [1mchild's[22m returned sum (it's already in the return register) and [38;5;10m%xmm1[0m holds the accumulator. x86 [38;5;10maddsd[0m returns the [3mdestination[23m operand, quieted, when the destination is a NaN — so the child's NaN sign and payload win over the accumulator's. Rust's [38;5;10msum += child[0m puts the accumulator in the destination, giving the opposite winner:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mroot=NaN(0x7ff8…000) child=-NaN(0xfff8…000)  →  C=0xfff8…000  Rust=0x7ff8…000
root=NaN(0x7ff8…000) child=sNaN(0x7ff0…001)  →  C=0x7ff8…001  Rust=0x7ff8…000
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Reachable from the public API, since [38;5;10madd_node[0m accepts an arbitrary [38;5;10mdouble[0m. I replaced the [38;5;10m+=[0m with an [38;5;10maddsd[0m helper that spells the hardware rule out explicitly (rather than writing [38;5;10mchild + sum[0m, which LLVM is free to commute because IR [38;5;10mfadd[0m is commutative).[0m[0m
